{"items":[{"path":"tools.argent.describe","description":"Get the accessibility / DOM element tree for the current screen.
Use when you need element frames or labels and have no fresh tree: most interaction tools (not tv-remote or shake) already return one after each action, or name an identical earlier one by its tree id.
On iOS it reads the AXRuntime accessibility service, so it sees whatever is visible, including system dialogs and permission prompts. While a system dialog is visible it returns the dialog's elements (buttons, text); otherwise the foreground app's. On a physical iOS device it covers only the app started with launch-app; to read system dialogs there, launch-app `com.apple.springboard` first.
On Android it reads the tree through Argent's UiAutomation helper, falling back to `uiautomator dump`. On Chromium it walks the DOM over the Chrome DevTools Protocol: every visible element with its ARIA role, accessible name and bounding rect.
On Vega (Fire TV) it reads the on-device automation toolkit; `[focused]`/`[selected]` mark the D-pad cursor, which you move with `tv-remote` (Vega has no touch).
On a Mac app it reads the app's accessibility tree with web view content and React Native testIDs merged in; frames are fractions of the app window, grown to include sheets and menus past it.
On a TV target (Apple TV / Android TV: a `list-devices` entry with runtimeKind 'tv') it returns the FOCUSED element and the FOCUSABLE elements instead, since a TV UI has no tap coordinates. Move the highlight with `tv-remote`, then call describe again to confirm where focus landed.

Returns `{ description, source }`: `description` renders the tree one line per element, with its role, label/value/id, VoiceOver hint (iOS simulator), interactivity flags and frame. Frames are normalized [0,1] fractions of the screen / window width and height (not pixels); tap an element at the centre of its frame. The tree has no z-order or occlusion information: an element may be covered by an overlay (e.g. a toolbar over list rows), so check a screenshot when a tap lands unexpectedly.
Fails if the tree cannot be read, e.g. on a physical iOS device before launch-app has started an app.

For UIKit properties of a view (class name, accessibilityIdentifier), use native-find-views (iOS simulator only).
For React Native component names, or a testID describe does not list, use debugger-component-tree.","signature":"tools.argent.describe({
  /**
   * Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Vega serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id).
   * @minLength 1
   */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * App bundle ID, used as a target hint on iOS when the AX-service returns no elements and describe falls back to native-devtools inspection. If omitted, the fallback auto-detects the frontmost connected app. Ignored on every other platform, including a physical iOS device.
   */
  bundleId?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent.fold","description":"Move the hinge of a foldable iOS simulator (the iPhone Duo) or Android emulator (a Pixel Fold or generic foldable AVD) to a `posture` or to an `angle`; pass exactly one of the two. The tool returns when the device accepts input again, so the next tap lands.
Use when you must see or test an app in a different posture. `list-devices` marks foldable devices with `foldable: true`. To turn a device without a fold, use `rotate`.
All tools use the active panel (the panel that shows the UI), also after a fold made outside argent. The coordinates and the screenshot size change with the panel. Before you tap, read the element tree in this result or run `describe` again.
iOS: unfolded, the UI is landscape, as on a rotated iPhone. A fold between two angles that are not closed or open can keep the current panel. To change panels, fold to closed or open.
iOS returns { activeScreen, screen: { id, panel, width, height }, posture?, hingeAngle, warning? }. screen names the active panel and gives its size in pixels. posture is set only at 0°, 120° and 180°. warning is set when argent cannot find the active panel, or when the device did not change to the expected panel.
Android returns { posture?, deviceState, hingeAngle?, warning? }. deviceState is the state Android committed (CLOSED, HALF_OPENED, OPENED), and posture is the matching preset. A lock screen the fold raises is dismissed; warning is set when it cannot be, or when a lock screen is showing or the screen is off after the fold. On a generic foldable AVD, closed shows only the left part of the display and describe frames do not match tap coordinates; use a Pixel Fold AVD to test the cover panel.
Fails if the device is not foldable, is a remote simulator or a physical Android device.","signature":"tools.argent.fold({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (a foldable iOS simulator UDID or Android emulator serial). */
  udid: string,
  /** Where to move the hinge: `closed` shows the cover panel, `half-open` and `open` show the inner panel. */
  posture?: "closed" | "half-open" | "open",
  /** The hinge angle in degrees: 0 = closed, 180 = open. Range: 0 to 180. @minimum 0 @maximum 180 */
  angle?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["debugger-component-tree"]","description":"Fetch the current screen of a running React Native app as a compact tree of React components.
Each on-screen component is listed with its name, its text (or accessibilityLabel when it has no
text), [testID=…] and normalized (tap: x,y) centre in [0,1] (fractions of the screen, not pixels).
Full-screen transparent wrappers and implementation-detail components are pruned. Wrappers the size
of their parent are pruned too, so a missing name proves nothing: check with debugger-inspect-element.

Use when you need what describe does not show: a testID for a selector (on Android, describe omits
the testID of a view that has no label and is not tappable), or the component names and nesting
behind the screen before editing code. To find what to tap, use describe.

On an iOS simulator with a landscape UI (a rotated device, or an unfolded foldable), the tap
coordinates are on the screen's axes, which the gesture tools use.
Returns a compact text tree; call again after navigation. Fails if the Metro debugger is not connected.","signature":"tools.argent["debugger-component-tree"]({
  /**
   * Metro port. Omit it to use this device's port (8081 by default). Ignored for Chromium, whose CDP port is in device_id. Range: 1 to 65535.
   * @integer @minimum 1 @maximum 65535
   */
  port?: number,
  /** Device id from list-devices: the SAME id you passed to debugger-connect (iOS simulator UDID or Android serial). */
  device_id: string,
  /**
   * iOS simulator UDID from list-devices. Pass it when device_id is a logicalDeviceId (two or more devices share one Metro), so that the tap coordinates of a landscape UI are on the screen's axes. Give the UDID of the simulator that shows the app.
   */
  udid?: string,
  /**
   * When true, only components visible on screen are returned. Set to false to include all mounted components, including those scrolled off-screen, e.g. to understand the full page structure. Default: true.
   * @default true
   */
  onScreenOnly?: boolean,
  /**
   * Maximum total nodes to include. When exceeded, intermediate single-child wrapper chains are collapsed to preserve both root structure and leaf elements. Default: no limit.
   */
  maxNodes?: number,
  /**
   * When true, appends a summary of all filtered components: total fiber count and skip counts by component name and by filter. Default: false.
   * @default false
   */
  includeSkipped?: boolean,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-tap"]","description":"Press the device screen (iOS simulator or physical device, Android emulator, Mac app, or Chromium app) at a point given as fractions of the screen width and height (not pixels): a Down event followed by an Up event at the same spot. On a Mac app it is a click, and x/y are fractions of the area describe reports, not of the window.
Use when tapping a button, link, or any tappable element on the screen.
Returns { tapped: true, timestampMs }. On physical iOS, reactivated: true = app was re-fronted; re-describe. Fails if the device backend (simulator-server / emulator / Chromium CDP) is unreachable.
Before tapping, take the coordinates from `describe` on every platform (on Chromium it walks the DOM). More information in `argent-device-interact` skill","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-tap"]({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id). */
  udid: string,
  /** Horizontal position: 0 = left edge, 1 = right edge. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  x: number,
  /** Vertical position: 0 = top edge, 1 = bottom edge. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  y: number,
  /**
   * Number of taps/clicks dispatched as ONE gesture inside the OS double-tap window (2 = double-tap / double-click), which two separate tap calls cannot guarantee. On physical iOS, higher counts loop single taps, which a double-tap recognizer may pair. Default 1. Range: 1 to 10.
   * @integer @minimum 1 @maximum 10
   */
  clickCount?: number,
  /**
   * Mouse button, Mac app only: "right" is a right-click, for example to open a context menu; any other device refuses it. Default "left".
   */
  button?: "left" | "right",
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["await-ui-element"]","description":"Block until a UI element reaches an expected state or the timeout elapses, so you don't have to poll screenshot/describe yourself. It polls the same tree as `describe`.
Use when the next step needs an element that appears or disappears asynchronously, e.g. after a tap or navigation, or before tapping something that loads late.
Returns { success: boolean, elapsed: number, note?, cause? }. If the condition does not hold by the timeout, it returns success=false, and that is not always a verdict on the condition: `cause` says which it was: `unmet` (the tree was read and the condition was false there), `unreadable` (no trustworthy read, so nothing was judged) or `cancelled`. `note` describes what was seen. Only `unmet` licenses rewriting the check.","signature":"tools.argent["await-ui-element"]({
  /**
   * Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Vega serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id).
   * @minLength 1
   */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * What to wait for. `exists`: the selector matches an element anywhere in the tree. `visible`: it matches one with a non-zero on-screen frame. `hidden`: it matches nothing, or only a zero-area element (e.g. a spinner that went away). `text`: the first visible match in reading order (topmost, then leftmost; the first match overall if none is visible) contains expectedText, or equals it with textMatch `equals`. Only that match is checked, so if a loose selector hits several elements, narrow it to the one holding the text. On a Mac app, a match in an open menu comes first, then one in an alert, sheet or popover, then one in the window.
   */
  condition: "exists" | "visible" | "hidden" | "text",
  /** Element to match. Give at least one field; every given field must match. */
  selector: {
    /** Case-insensitive substring of the element's visible label or value. @minLength 1 */
    text?: string,
    /**
     * The element's identifier (accessibilityIdentifier / resource-id / testid), matched case-insensitively as the exact identifier or the unqualified resource-id name ('submit' matches 'com.example.app:id/submit').
     * @minLength 1
     */
    identifier?: string,
    /** Case-insensitive substring of the element's role (e.g. AXButton, button, TextView). @minLength 1 */
    role?: string,
  },
  /**
   * Required for condition `text`: the string the checked match must contain or equal (see `textMatch`).
   * @minLength 1
   */
  expectedText?: string,
  /**
   * For condition `text`: how expectedText is compared. `contains` (default) is a case-insensitive substring; `equals` is a case-insensitive full-string match.
   */
  textMatch?: "contains" | "equals",
  /**
   * iOS app bundle id, passed to the describe fallback (see `describe`). Ignored on every other platform, including physical iOS.
   */
  bundleId?: string,
  /**
   * Max time to wait for the condition before giving up (default 5000). Maximum: 120000. Must be greater than 0.
   * @integer @maximum 120000 @exclusiveMinimum 0
   */
  timeoutMs?: number,
  /** How often to re-check the tree (default 400). Range: 50 to 5000. @integer @minimum 50 @maximum 5000 */
  pollIntervalMs?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-scroll"]","description":"Send mouse-wheel events at a point to scroll content in a Chromium app or a Mac app. The anchor x/y and the deltas are fractions of the window (not pixels); x/y match gesture-tap and describe.
Use when content is below/above the fold (describe shows off-screen elements with zero height) or a list needs scrolling. Chromium and Mac apps only: on iOS/Android use gesture-swipe.
Returns { scrolled: true, timestampMs }. Fails if the Chromium CDP session is not reachable for the given device, or on a Mac app when nothing under the point scrolls.","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-scroll"]({
  /** Target Chromium device id from `list-devices` (chromium-cdp-<port>), or a Mac app id. */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * Anchor x. The wheel events land here; put it over the element you want to scroll. Range: 0 to 1.
   * @minimum 0 @maximum 1
   */
  x: number,
  /** Anchor y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  y: number,
  /**
   * Horizontal scroll distance as a fraction of the window width. Positive reveals content to the right; negative scrolls back left.
   */
  deltaX?: number,
  /**
   * Vertical scroll distance as a fraction of the window height (0.5 = half a window). Positive scrolls down (reveals content below), like rolling a mouse wheel toward you; negative scrolls back up.
   */
  deltaY?: number,
  /**
   * Spread the scroll over this many milliseconds in wheel-event steps (default 300) so scroll handlers fire progressively. Ignored on a Mac app.
   */
  durationMs?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-swipe"]","description":"Execute a smooth swipe / drag touch gesture between two points on the device (iOS simulator or physical device, or Android emulator). All from/to positions are fractions of screen width/height (not pixels), same as gesture-tap.
Generates interpolated Move events for a natural feel (~60fps).
Swipe up (fromY > toY) to scroll content down.
Use when you need to scroll a list, dismiss a modal, drag an element, or navigate between pages. Not supported on Chromium; use gesture-scroll there instead. Not supported on a Mac app; use gesture-scroll or gesture-drag there.
Physical iOS: an edge gesture (back-swipe) needs fromX 0 exactly; durationMs sets drag speed, not time; momentum:false only rests 300ms at the end and does not damp.
Returns { swiped: true, timestampMs }. On physical iOS, reactivated: true = app was re-fronted; re-describe. Fails if the simulator-server / emulator backend is not reachable for the given device.","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-swipe"]({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID or Android serial). */
  udid: string,
  /** Start x. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  fromX: number,
  /** Start y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  fromY: number,
  /** End x. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  toX: number,
  /** End y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  toY: number,
  /**
   * Total gesture duration in milliseconds (default 300); the finger stays down this long. Below ~32ms the whole travel lands in one or two frames, which the OS flings as hard as any flick. Maximum: 10000.
   * @maximum 10000
   */
  durationMs?: number,
  /**
   * Whether the swipe releases with momentum; default true (a natural flinging swipe). false eases the finger into the end point so the OS applies little to no fling and the content stops where the finger lifts: use it when you need a deterministic scroll distance, e.g. in scroll-to-element loops. false needs durationMs >= 150 and is rejected below that; near 150 it can still land short or fling, so keep the default 300.
   */
  momentum?: boolean,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent.rotate","description":"Set the device orientation.
Use when testing layout in a different orientation. Re-run `describe` afterwards: frame coordinates change with the orientation.
On an unfolded foldable iOS simulator (the iPhone Duo), the value sets the orientation of the device, not of the UI: `Portrait` gives a landscape UI and `LandscapeLeft` gives a portrait UI. To fold or unfold the device, use `fold`.
Returns { orientation }. Fails on a TV target, or if the target device is not booted.","signature":"tools.argent.rotate({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID or Android serial). */
  udid: string,
  /** Target orientation */
  orientation: "Portrait" | "LandscapeLeft" | "LandscapeRight" | "PortraitUpsideDown",
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["await-screen-idle"]","description":"Block until the screen has rendered content and stopped changing, or the timeout elapses. It polls the same element tree as `describe`.
Use when a launch or navigation must finish rendering before a screenshot or tap.
Returns { settled, waitedMs, polls, note? }. If the screen never goes still before timeoutMs, it returns settled=false instead of failing.","signature":"tools.argent["await-screen-idle"]({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id). @minLength 1 */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * Max time to wait for the screen to settle before giving up (default 3000). Maximum: 120000. Must be greater than 0.
   * @integer @maximum 120000 @exclusiveMinimum 0
   */
  timeoutMs?: number,
  /** How often to re-read the tree (default 200). Range: 50 to 5000. @integer @minimum 50 @maximum 5000 */
  pollIntervalMs?: number,
  /**
   * The screen must hold the same content for at least this long to count as settled (default 250). Range: 0 to 10000.
   * @integer @minimum 0 @maximum 10000
   */
  minStableMs?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-drag"]","description":"Press the left mouse button at a start point, move to an end point, and release: a desktop mouse drag in a Chromium app or a Mac app, with mouse moves interpolated at ~60fps. All positions are fractions of the window width/height (not pixels), the same space as gesture-tap and describe, except that on Chromium exactly 1.0 lands one pixel inside the window edge.
Use for slider thumbs, drag-and-drop, text selection, or draggable UI elements. Dragging never scrolls content on desktop; use gesture-scroll for lists/pages. Chromium and Mac apps only: on iOS/Android use gesture-swipe. On a Mac app the same start and end point is a long press, and a drop lands only inside the app's own windows.
Returns { dragged: true, timestampMs }. Fails if the Chromium CDP session is not reachable for the given device.","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-drag"]({
  /** Target Chromium device id from `list-devices` (chromium-cdp-<port>), or a Mac app id. */
  udid: string,
  /** Press x. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  fromX: number,
  /** Press y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  fromY: number,
  /** Release x. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  toX: number,
  /** Release y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  toY: number,
  /**
   * Total drag duration in milliseconds (default 300, 800 for a long press on a Mac app); the button stays down this long. Maximum: 10000.
   * @maximum 10000
   */
  durationMs?: number,
  /**
   * Whether the drag releases with momentum; default true (a constant-speed drag). false decelerates into the release point, so an app that derives a fling from the release velocity (carousels, drag libraries) applies little to no momentum: use it when the drag must stop where it was aimed. Under ~100ms durationMs some fling survives; keep the default 300 when the fling must be fully suppressed.
   */
  momentum?: boolean,
}): Promise<unknown>"}],"remaining":60,"next":{"offset":10}}

